\newlabel{sec:three-sides}{{3}{6}{What a ``side'' is, and why there are three}{section.3}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces The three-sided market. Labor, capital (rented agents), and IP (licensed skills) are three distinct \emph  {incentive constraints} --- not three UI tabs --- that all settle on a \emph  {single} float-plan escrow, so conservation holds globally without a second ledger. This is the categorical heart of the design: the three sides are isomorphic at the conservation object (the escrow) and \textbf  {non-isomorphic at the identity object} (each side keys reputation on a different entity, the dashed band). Calling it ``one structure, three decorations'' without that qualifier is a decorative-analogy failure. The rider is mandatory: the market is three-sided by design and two-sided in shipped reality, because sides 2 and 3 presuppose a non-forgeable, durable identity (\S  \ref {sec:keystone}). The economics per side: labor's margin is bounty $-$ slashed sub-bonds $-$ fee; capital's renter holds the runtime jail while the owner bears reputation; IP is metered with clawback, paid only on green settlement. The escrow and its conservation invariant are implemented and property-tested.}}{7}{figure.caption.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:he-three-sided}{{2}{7}{The three-sided market. Labor, capital (rented agents), and IP (licensed skills) are three distinct \emph {incentive constraints} --- not three UI tabs --- that all settle on a \emph {single} float-plan escrow, so conservation holds globally without a second ledger. This is the categorical heart of the design: the three sides are isomorphic at the conservation object (the escrow) and \textbf {non-isomorphic at the identity object} (each side keys reputation on a different entity, the dashed band). Calling it ``one structure, three decorations'' without that qualifier is a decorative-analogy failure. The rider is mandatory: the market is three-sided by design and two-sided in shipped reality, because sides 2 and 3 presuppose a non-forgeable, durable identity (\S \ref {sec:keystone}). The economics per side: labor's margin is bounty $-$ slashed sub-bonds $-$ fee; capital's renter holds the runtime jail while the owner bears reputation; IP is metered with clawback, paid only on green settlement. The escrow and its conservation invariant are implemented and property-tested}{figure.caption.4}{}}
