\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {10.3}The grading-oracle keystone}{22}{subsection.10.3}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {11}{\ignorespaces The grading-oracle incentive-compatibility problem --- the hole under the core theorem. Every folk-theorem claim in the layer bottoms out in ``the agent picks the work, the daemon derives the grade.'' But a folk theorem is only as good as the \emph  {public signal} it conditions on (Green--Porter 1984; Abreu--Pearce--Stacchetti 1990 require the signal distribution to be common knowledge and \emph  {exogenous}). The grade is produced by a capturable evaluator, so the signal is endogenous and the folk theorem does not apply as stated. We resolve it two ways: ground settlement in \emph  {machine-checkable} oracles the agent cannot author (Fix A, strategy-proof by construction), and \emph  {bond-and- slash} the human/LLM judges where machine-checking is impossible (Fix B), then require the rate-the-raters recursion to terminate at a machine-checkable base.}}{22}{figure.caption.22}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:he-grading-oracle}{{11}{22}{The grading-oracle incentive-compatibility problem --- the hole under the core theorem. Every folk-theorem claim in the layer bottoms out in ``the agent picks the work, the daemon derives the grade.'' But a folk theorem is only as good as the \emph {public signal} it conditions on (Green--Porter 1984; Abreu--Pearce--Stacchetti 1990 require the signal distribution to be common knowledge and \emph {exogenous}). The grade is produced by a capturable evaluator, so the signal is endogenous and the folk theorem does not apply as stated. We resolve it two ways: ground settlement in \emph {machine-checkable} oracles the agent cannot author (Fix A, strategy-proof by construction), and \emph {bond-and- slash} the human/LLM judges where machine-checking is impossible (Fix B), then require the rate-the-raters recursion to terminate at a machine-checkable base}{figure.caption.22}{}}
\newlabel{thm:folk}{{10.1}{22}{The folk theorem needs an exogenous signal}{theorem.10.1}{}}
\citation{zheng2023}
